{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Before HaShem shall you be purified.</b> For Yom Kippur does not atone except for penitents (Shavuot 13a), and before HaShem atones upon them, every person is obligated to purify himself from the sole of the foot to the head from all the impurity of sin; therefore it says: ״לִפְנֵי ה׳ תִּטְהָרוּ״, and לִפְנֵי functions in the sense of קֹדֶם, 'before.' And something greater than this I found in the Book of the Zohar on the verse ״מִפְּנֵי שֵׂיבָה תָּקוּם״ (Leviticus 19:32) — that before the time of old age a person should arise from the slumber of his trance to return to HaShem; all the more so does לִפְנֵי bear the sense of 'before.' That is: before the coming of the great and awesome day of HaShem, purify yourselves by means of repentance, and afterward HaShem will purify you by means of atonement. And He further warned concerning the inward, heartfelt repentance that is 'before HaShem' — for the matter entrusted to the heart is revealed to none but HaShem alone — so that one not be among the dyed-fast, the fasting ones who multiply supplications in order to be reckoned as righteous, while he is a generation pure in the eyes of creatures yet from his inner filth he has not washed. Therefore it says: ״לִפְנֵי ה׳ תִּטְהָרוּ״." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Yoma 85b) expounded: specifically transgressions that are לִפְנֵי ה׳ — that is, between a person and the Omnipresent — Yom Kippur atones for, but not for transgressions between a person and his fellow. Therefore it says ״וְעַל כָּל עַם הַקָּהָל יְכַפֵּר״ — meaning, specifically when they become one congregation, to be in a single band, to be as one to serve HaShem — then the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts their repentance; and not at a time when their hearts are divided. And since it is the way of the world that on Yom Kippur people seek forgiveness one from another, yet on the morrow they return to their former state, therefore it says (Exodus 35:1) ״וַיַּקְהֵל מֹשֶׁה״ — on the morrow of Yom Kippur he made them into one congregation, so that they would remain so all the days of the year. And since all words of strife are drawn from the dealings that are between a person and his fellow, [Moses] sat to judge the people on the morrow of Yom Kippur, for through this they would be one congregation and the entire people each in his place would come in peace. And for this reason this day is called ״שַׁבַּת שַׁבָּתוֹן״, for cessation of business and rest is a great cause toward peace, and the doubling of the rest is because every person has an outward and an inward movement and commotion: the outward, through the tools of labor in every manner of work; and the inward, which comes from the fat and the blood that surge in pursuit of desires. And on the Sabbath one rests only from the outward, but on this holy day one rests from both, for there is no eating and drinking either; therefore it is called ״שַׁבַּת שַׁבָּתוֹן״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:05.099577968Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}